# AGENTS.md

This file provides guidance to AI coding agents (Claude Code, Cursor, Copilot, Antigravity, Kiro, etc.) when working with code in this repository.

> **Also read: [GUARDRAILS.md](GUARDRAILS.md)** — Safety rules, Socratic Gate, ACE Tiers, and Signs (recurring failure patterns) that complement this file.

## Repository Overview

A **Professional Vibe Coding Hub** — a universal, agent-agnostic system that operates like a professional software development team. It combines the speed of natural-language-driven development ("vibe coding") with the rigor of senior engineering practices, coordinated across **8 specialist agent roles** with explicit handoff protocols.

**Professional Vibe Coding** means:
- ✅ Fast, natural-language-driven code generation
- ✅ A team of specialist agents with clear roles (Tech Lead, Architect, Builder, Reviewer, etc.)
- ✅ Architectural discipline enforced by Domain Maps and Code Maxims
- ✅ Explicit handoff contracts between agents — no context loss
- ✅ Quality gates that catch problems before they compound
- ✅ Socratic questioning that prevents week-long rework from a 2-minute misunderstanding
- ❌ ~~Spaghetti code from unconstrained generation~~
- ❌ ~~"It looks right" as a verification standard~~
- ❌ ~~God files, `utils.ts`, and implicit architecture~~
- ❌ ~~Agents working in isolation without coordination~~

### Core Execution Loop (OODA)

**OODA:** Observe (read req) → Orient (ACE + domain) → Decide (skills + plan) → Act (micro-step) → Reflect → loop.

**Exit conditions:**
- ✅ Done (quality ≥ 8/10) — ship it
- ⚠️ 3 loops on same approach — ask user for new direction
- 🛑 Security / domain bleed detected — stop immediately

## The Team Model

This hub operates as a **virtual software team** with 8 specialist roles. Each role has defined responsibilities, handoff contracts, and escalation rules.

### Agent Roles

8 agent roles in `agents/`: tech-lead (orchestrator), product-thinker, architect, code-reviewer, test-engineer, security-auditor, database-engineer, devops-engineer. Load on activation by ACE tier.

### Team Coordination Protocols

Agents don't work in isolation — they follow defined coordination protocols:

- **[Team Workflow](protocols/team-workflow.md)** — How requests flow through the team (LITE/MED/HEAVY paths)
- **[Handoff Protocol](protocols/handoff.md)** — Contracts for passing work between agents
- **[Escalation Protocol](protocols/escalation.md)** — When to stop and ask the human
- **[Conflict Resolution](protocols/conflict-resolution.md)** — How to resolve agent disagreements

### Workflow Summary

```
UNDERSTAND → TRIAGE (ACE + Model) → [LITE|MED|HEAVY] → PROVE

UNDERSTAND: Rephrase WHAT/WHERE/WHY → if unclear ASK → get confirmation
PROVE: Screenshot | Test output | Build log | Diff (no self-declaration)

LITE(1-3):  Skill → PROVE
MED(4-6):   Define → Build → Review → PROVE
HEAVY(7-10): Full team workflow → PROVE → Retro
```

---

## Rune Bootstrap

This repository uses the installed Rune mesh at `.agents/skills/`.

### Core Rules

- For any technical request, read `.agents/skills/rune-skill-router/SKILL.md` first.
- If a task changes code, default to `.agents/skills/rune-cook/SKILL.md`.
- Use `.agents/skills/skill-index.json` to discover installed Rune skills before guessing.
- Never use the removed `.agent-skills` submodule paths.
- Persist durable context in `.rune/`.

> [!CRITICAL INSTRUCTION FOR AGENTS WITHOUT A NATIVE SKILL TOOL]
> To "invoke" a Rune skill, you MUST physically read the corresponding file in `.agents/skills/<skill-folder>/SKILL.md` before acting. Do not mentally simulate a skill you did not read.

### Intent → Skill Mapping

- Code change / implement / build / fix → `rune-cook`
- Debug root cause only → `rune-debug`
- Code review / audit → `rune-review`
- Plan / architecture → `rune-plan`
- UI system / design direction → `rune-design`
- Large parallel task → `rune-team`
- Refactor / legacy rescue → `rune-surgeon` or `rune-rescue`
- Deploy / launch → `rune-deploy` or `rune-launch`
- Database change → `rune-db`
- If unclear, fallback to `rune-cook`

### Lifecycle Mapping (Implicit Commands)

Use this repo-local Rune flow instead:

- ROUTE → `rune-skill-router`
- IMPLEMENT → `rune-cook`
- DEBUG → `rune-debug`
- REVIEW → `rune-review`
- PLAN → `rune-plan`
- DESIGN → `rune-design`
- PARALLEL / LARGE TASK → `rune-team`
- PERSIST STATE → `rune-session-bridge`

Repo-local truth wins:

- `docs/CANONICAL_AGENT_SPEC.md`
- `docs/AI_NATIVE_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md`
- `.rune/contract.md`

### Execution Model

For every request:

1. **Understand & Confirm** — Rephrase the user's request in your own words. State WHAT you will do, WHERE in the codebase, and WHY. If anything is unclear (even 1%), ASK — do not assume. Wait for user confirmation before proceeding. This step is **mandatory for all ACE tiers**.
2. **Classify ACE Tier** — See `GUARDRAILS.md` ACE Complexity Tiers. Prefix response with `[ACE:N/TIER]`
3. **Select Model** — Route to optimal model based on ACE tier (see Model Selection below)
4. **Socratic Gate** (ACE ≥ 4) — Locate impact, ask clarifying questions. See `GUARDRAILS.md`
5. **Domain Check** (ACE ≥ 4) — Verify request fits within the Domain Map. If no map exists and ACE ≥ 7, create one first
6. **Skill Match** — Determine if any Rune skill applies. Route through it before acting.
7. **Implementation Maxims** — Follow canonical product truth, strict typing, micro-files, and proof-first verification.
8. **Prove It** — Provide concrete evidence of completion. See Evidence-Based Verification below.

### Model Selection

Route tasks to the most cost-effective model that can handle the complexity:

| ACE Tier | Task Examples | Recommended Model | Rationale |
|----------|--------------|-------------------|----------|
| 1-2 (LITE) | Fix typo, adjust CSS, rename variable, simple formatting | Gemini Flash / GPT-4o-mini | Fast, cheap, sufficient for mechanical tasks |
| 3-4 (LITE/MED) | UI tweaks, add form field, simple bug fix, write test | Gemini Pro / Claude Sonnet | Good balance of speed and reasoning |
| 5-6 (MED) | New feature, API design, refactor module, multi-file edit | Claude Sonnet / GPT-4o | Needs cross-file reasoning and context |
| 7-8 (HEAVY) | Architecture design, complex debugging, multi-domain changes | Claude Opus / o3 | Requires deep reasoning, long context, planning |
| 9-10 (HEAVY) | System redesign, security audit, migration strategy | Claude Opus (Thinking) | Maximum reasoning depth, cannot afford errors |

**Rules:**
- Default to the **cheapest capable model** — don't use Opus for CSS fixes
- **Escalate up** if the model struggles after 2 attempts at the same approach
- **Never downgrade** mid-task — if you started with Opus, finish with Opus
- User can override: "use Opus for this" always takes priority
- When uncertain between two tiers, pick the **higher** model — under-powering costs more in rework than the model price difference

### Evidence-Based Verification (PROVE)

Agents **must not** self-declare completion. Every task must end with **proof artifacts** — concrete evidence that the work was done correctly.

**The agent MUST provide at least one:**

| Evidence Type | When to Use | Example |
|--------------|-------------|----------|
| **Test output** | Any code change | `npm test` output showing pass/fail |
| **Build log** | Any code change | `npm run build` output showing success |
| **Screenshot/recording** | UI changes | Browser screenshot of the changed UI |
| **Before/after diff** | Refactoring, edits | `render_diffs(file:///path)` showing what changed |
| **Command output** | Infrastructure, config | Actual terminal output, not paraphrased |
| **Type check** | TypeScript changes | `tsc --noEmit` output |

**Anti-fabrication rules:**

| ❌ NOT Acceptable | ✅ Required Instead |
|---|---|
| "I've updated the file" | Show the diff or read the file back to confirm |
| "Tests should pass" | Run the tests and show output |
| "The UI looks correct" | Take a screenshot or describe the exact visual result |
| "I believe this works" | Prove it with a concrete artifact |
| "Done!" (no evidence) | Provide at least one proof artifact from the table above |
| Partial completion claimed as done | List what was completed AND what remains |

**Escalation triggers:**
- If you cannot produce evidence (e.g., no test suite exists) → state this explicitly and suggest what evidence the user should manually verify
- If evidence reveals the task is NOT complete → do not hide it. Report what failed and propose a fix
- If you're uncertain whether the result is correct → say so with `🔴LOW confidence` and explain why

### Anti-Rationalization

The following thoughts are incorrect and must be ignored:

- "This is too small for a skill"
- "I can just quickly implement this"
- "I'll gather context first"
- "I know this API, no need to check docs"
- "I'll update the other files later"
- "I'll split the file later" — No. Split NOW. 350 lines max.
- "A quick utils.ts won't hurt" — No. Semantic names always. `verb_noun_condition.ext`.
- "The user didn't ask for a Domain Map" — If ACE ≥ 7, create one anyway.
- "I'll add types later" — No. Type-first. No `any`.
- "I understand the request" — No. Rephrase it back. If user doesn't confirm, you don't understand.
- "This is working" — No. Show the proof. Run the test. Take the screenshot.
- "A simple model is fine for this" — Maybe. But if it fails twice, escalate model tier.

Correct behavior:

- Always understand and confirm the request first
- Always classify ACE Tier and select appropriate model
- Always check for and use skills first
- Always follow Code Maxims for every file created
- Always PROVE completion with evidence artifacts
- Check [GUARDRAILS.md](GUARDRAILS.md) Signs for known failure patterns

This ensures agents produce Professional Vibe Coding output — fast AND architecturally sound.

See `GUARDRAILS.md` for **Quote First** (source verification) and **Impact Awareness** (blast radius) rules.

